1. Definition and religious significance
Kālīkula is a name used for Śākta Tantric traditions centered on Kālī and related forms of the Goddess. The term combines Kālī, the Goddess, with kula, meaning family, lineage, group, or, in certain Tantric philosophical contexts, a larger interconnected whole.

Kālīkula is not one universally standardized organization with a single scripture, ritual system, or theology. It is better understood as a broad family of related traditions whose teachings developed across different regions and historical periods. Some are strongly associated with Bengal and eastern India; others belong to the medieval Tantric traditions of Kashmir, particularly the Krama current. 

