In these traditions, Kālī is not merely a goddess who destroys evil. Depending on the text and lineage, she can represent supreme consciousness, the power of time, the dynamic nature of existence, the dissolution of the universe, or the reality beyond ordinary distinctions.

The significance of Kālī therefore extends beyond her visual imagery. Her iconography, mythology, mantra traditions, and philosophical interpretations provide different ways of understanding the relationship between consciousness, creation, time, transformation, and liberation.

2. The philosophical foundations of Kālīkula
2.1 Śakti as supreme reality
Śakti means power, capacity, or divine energy. In Śākta traditions, Śakti is not simply an instrument belonging to a separate male deity. She may be understood as the ultimate reality itself—the power through which existence manifests, continues, and returns to its source.

In some nondual Tantric systems, Śiva and Śakti are inseparable aspects of reality. Śiva represents consciousness, while Śakti represents its dynamic, self-expressive power. This distinction is a philosophical framework, not necessarily a claim that the two are separate entities.

Kālīkula traditions vary in how they formulate this relationship. Some emphasize Kālī as the supreme Goddess beyond all categories; others explain her through more elaborate systems of manifestation, cognition, and dissolution.

2.2 Kālī and time
The Sanskrit word kāla is associated with time and, in certain contexts, death. Kālī's relationship with time is therefore a central theme in many interpretations of her name and mythology.

In the Kashmirian Krama tradition, the Goddess is associated with cycles of manifestation, experience, and dissolution. A particularly important doctrine concerns the twelve Kālīs, whose sequence is interpreted in later philosophical texts as the unfolding and reabsorption of consciousness. 

