2.3 Creation and dissolution
Kālī's destructive imagery is often interpreted through the larger cycle of existence.

Manifestation: reality appears in differentiated forms.

Experience: beings encounter and participate in the world.

Transformation: forms change and cease to remain as they were.

Dissolution: distinctions and manifested forms return to their source.

These stages are a useful interpretive framework, not a single four-stage doctrine shared by every Kālīkula lineage. Particular Tantric texts employ their own technical classifications.

2.4 Liberation and recognition
Some nondual Śākta and Śaiva traditions understand liberation as a transformation in how reality is recognized and experienced. The practitioner seeks more than temporary supernatural benefits or favorable circumstances.

In the Kashmirian Krama tradition, the sequence of the Goddess can be interpreted as the unfolding and reabsorption of awareness. The aim is associated with realizing the nature of consciousness rather than remaining confined to ordinary, limited perception. 

