5. Mantra and the subtle form of the Goddess
Mantra is central to Śrīvidyā. In its theological framework, a mantra is not simply a verbal description of the Goddess; it is understood as a form through which her power and presence are encountered.

The Śrīvidyā mantra traditions include the well-known Pañcadaśī and Ṣoḍaśī systems. Their names refer to fifteenfold and sixteenfold mantra structures, respectively, though their exact forms and interpretations vary across lineages.

A distinction is necessary between discussing these systems academically and publishing restricted mantra instructions. Some forms of Śrīvidyā mantra practice are transmitted through initiation, and details differ among lineages.

For a public research archive, it is appropriate to explain the history, terminology, theological role, and textual context of mantra without presenting every lineage-specific practice as universally available.

6. Ritual, meditation, and liberation
Śrīvidyā has developed a range of practices that can include external worship, mantra recitation, yantra worship, visualization, meditation, and philosophical contemplation.

Their significance varies across historical periods and communities. Some ritual systems emphasize the performance of prescribed external rites; others give particular importance to internalized worship and the realization of the Goddess within consciousness.

Modern scholarship has traced the development of Śrīvidyā from early ritual systems toward later philosophical interpretations that place greater emphasis on liberation during one's lifetime, known as jīvanmukti. This is a historical development documented in particular textual traditions, not a claim that every Śrīvidyā lineage follows an identical path. 

