Śrīvidyā therefore encompasses more than ritual prosperity or the acquisition of worldly benefits. Its textual traditions also address knowledge, the nature of reality, devotion, and spiritual liberation.

7. Textual sources and historical research
The following sources are useful starting points for a Śrīvidyā research collection:

Vāmakeśvarīmata: An important early text associated with the development of the Tripurasundarī cult.

Yoginīhṛdaya: A major Śrīvidyā text that explores the theological and ritual significance of the Śrīcakra, mantra, and the Goddess.

Nityāṣoḍaśikārṇava: A foundational text associated with the Nityā tradition and the broader textual history of Śrīvidyā.

Paraśurāma Kalpasūtra: A ritual source associated with Śrīvidyā practice in particular traditions.

Lalitā Sahasranāma: A widely revered hymn of a thousand names of Lalitā, important to devotional and theological presentations of the Goddess.

These texts belong to different literary genres and historical contexts. They should not be treated as identical sources or assumed to have the same authority in every lineage. 

