8. What Śrīkula should not be reduced to
Śrīkula is not merely a tradition of geometric diagrams.

The Śrīcakra is not just decorative sacred geometry; it has a specific theological and ritual context.

Śrīvidyā is not exclusively a public devotional tradition, nor is it uniformly secretive.

All Śrīvidyā lineages do not share identical ritual procedures or philosophical interpretations.

Tripurasundarī should not be reduced to a symbol of physical beauty or worldly prosperity alone.

A scholarly archive should preserve the relationship between iconography, ritual, philosophy, historical texts, and living practice.

