Are they opposites?
Not in any absolute sense.

A common modern simplification portrays Kālīkula as fierce, dark, and destructive, while Śrīkula is portrayed as gentle, bright, and creative. The imagery can reflect real differences between particular forms of the Goddess, but the division becomes misleading when treated as a universal philosophical rule.

Kālī-centered traditions can be deeply contemplative and concerned with ultimate reality. Śrīvidyā traditions can involve complex esoteric ritual, rigorous discipline, and sophisticated metaphysics.

The two streams also share a wider Śākta framework in which the Goddess is central to understanding reality, ritual, and spiritual transformation. Their histories are interconnected, and their boundaries do not always correspond to a neat division between separate, mutually exclusive systems.

