Kālīgaṇa (कालीगण)
A Terminological and Historical Inquiry
Kālīgaṇa is a term requiring further textual and historical verification within the study of Śākta and Tantric traditions.

The Sanskrit compound can be examined through the words Kālī, the Goddess central to numerous Śākta traditions, and gaṇa, meaning a group, class, collection, or assembly. This linguistic interpretation provides a starting point for investigation, but does not by itself establish Kālīgaṇa as the formal name of a distinct Tantric school.

A closely related and well-documented area of study is Kālīkula, the family of Kālī-centered Śākta traditions. Within this wider field, the Kālīkrama and Krama traditions preserve important teachings concerning the Goddess, consciousness, time, and the cycles of manifestation and dissolution.

The doctrine of the twelve Kālīs is a particularly significant subject in this context. Its development is discussed in Tantric textual traditions and in the philosophical works of Kashmirian Śaiva authors, including Abhinavagupta and Kṣemarāja.

